11980	sWe looked at a stoat, and begin to admire its fearless nature, hunting animals much larger than itself head on. This kind of action is what we need on the corporate ladder.
11981	sGondolas, rare creatures that choose to simply observe instead of act have quite a set of interesting qualities to take advantage of. You know. Zen, peace, happiness... shrugging off a raging plasma fire...
11982	sFor whatever reason, when the body rejects DNA, the DNA goes sour, ending up as some kind of fly-like DNA jumble.
11983	sFoxes are now quite rare because of the \u0022fox ears\u0022 craze back in 2555. I mean, also because we're spacefarers who destroyed foxes' natural habitats ages ago, but that applies to most animals.
11984	sSo first they mixed moth and roach DNA to make mothroaches, and now we mix mothroach DNA with humanoids to make mothmen hybrids?
11985	sTurns out infusing most humanoids with lizard DNA creates features remarkably similar to those of lizardpeople. What a strange coincidence.
11986	sEVERYONE CALM DOWN! I'm not implying anything with this entry. Are we really so surprised that felinids are humans with mixed feline DNA?
11987	sHonestly, there wasn't much to gleam from a penguin's DNA, other than they walk funny.
11988	sHydroponics research has long been interested in splicing human DNA into plant DNA, creating podpeople. Many scientists didn't want to let those hippies get the leg up on them, so they attempted the opposite - to pretty uninteresting results.
